----- stdout -----
A fast test runner for Python suites.

Flags mirror pytest's. Anything after `--` goes to the framework unchanged. `aye config` shows the
resolved configuration and where each value came from. `aye show` shows the last run's results
again, and any test's details, without running anything (`aye show --help`).

Usage: aye [OPTIONS] [TEST]... [-- <ARGS>...]

Arguments:
  [TEST]...
          Files, directories, test ids (`path::Class::test[param]`), dotted names
          (`pkg.mod.Class.test`) or test keys

  [ARGS]...
          Arguments for the framework

Options:
  -k <EXPR>
          Only run tests matching the keyword expression

  -m <EXPR>
          Only run tests matching the tag expression (pytest's markers)

  -x, --exitfirst
          Stop after the first failure (`--maxfail 1`)

      --maxfail <N>
          Stop after N failures; 0 means no limit

  -v, --verbose...
          More output, in either format; repeat for more (up to -vvv)

  -q, --quiet...
          Less output: only the summary

  -s
          Don't capture output

      --lf
          Rerun only the tests that failed last time (all, if none did)
          
          [alias: --last-failed]

      --ff
          Run last time's failures first
          
          [alias: --failed-first]

  -n, --workers <N|auto>
          Worker processes, or `auto` for one per CPU

      --collect-only
          List the selected tests without running them
          
          [alias: --co]

      --no-refine
          Don't import files whose tests can't be known statically

      --continue-on-collection-errors
          Run the tests even when some files fail to collect

      --strict
          Treat collection warnings as errors

      --output <FORMAT>
          Output format; -v and -q set how much it shows

          Possible values:
          - text: For people: quiet on success, readable failures
          - json: JSON lines: the summary with -q, bad outcomes, failures in full with -v (for
            coding agents), every result with -vv, the raw event stream with -vvv

      --junitxml <PATH>
          Also write a JUnit XML report
          
          [alias: --junit-xml]

      --python <PATH>
          The Python interpreter that runs the tests

  -c, --config-file <FILE>
          The framework's config file (pytest's `-c`)

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --native
          Accepted and does nothing: aye's own engine always runs pytest's tests, without pytest
          (this was the prototype's switch)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

